  "result": "I've written the revised record to `record.jsonl`. It hasn't been through the gate yet, so it's unconfirmed until the harness validates it and the coder rebuilds the `blind` witness.\n\n**Why it failed:** the planned `blind` scenario hid only `rel.surface_gap`. Core computes that gap from both vehicles' footprints, so hiding the supplied value changes nothing. The only way to make the gap unknown was to drop a vehicle's size (`kin.extent`). That also left its lane overlap (`rel.occupies`) unknown. The rule needs that overlap at the same moment, so the verdict came back unknown because of `rel.occupies`, which wasn't hidden. No trace can make the gap the only unknown input.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **`blind` scenario only.** It now hides `[\"kin.speed\", \"kin.velocity\"]`. The new narrative is \"as `forbid`, with every footprint observed, but the source observes neither speeds nor velocities.\" That leaves the moving check (`drv.motion.moving`) and the hazard bound (gap ≤ speed × critical gap) unknown, so whether the law applies at all is unknown. Lane overlap and the gap still come from position, heading and size, which stay observed.\n- **Why two atoms, not the one the coder suggested:** the coder proposed hiding `kin.speed` alone. If Core works out speed from velocity, the way it works out the gap from footprints, that would fail in the same way. Hiding both means any unknown that turns up is one of the hidden atoms.\n- **One small fix:** the `s3` narrowing was missing a `kind`, so I set it to `reading`.\n\nEverything else is as planned: the element mappings, the two standards and how they're measured, the parameters, the applicability and obligation text, and the other five scenarios. The coder's rule and its other witnesses need no change.",
